 "issues": [
  {
   "id": "V1",
   "path": "sections.analysis.3",
   "category": "causality",
   "severity": "fix",
   "problem": "The paragraph says that each of the author's two imagined arrangements would lose both things: the visible difference in rank and the complete first-person account (EN: \"each would have cost a visible difference in rank and a complete first-person account\"; ZH: “都会少掉……高低位差和一段……自述”). That does not hold. In the first arrangement, where the General and the President face each other and speak in turn, the General still speaks, so the first-person account survives. That arrangement loses only the difference in rank. The no-speech arrangement is the one that loses the account. The counterfactual consequences are stated as a blanket conclusion that the paragraph's own logic does not support.",
   "evidence": [
    {
     "id": "U-E3",
     "quote": "he is to bow to Congress, which they are to return by uncovering without bowing"
    }
   ],
   "proposed": "Give each consequence to its own arrangement. ZH: “前者会少掉看得见的高低位差，后者会少掉一段完整记录在案的自述”. EN: \"the first would have lost the visible difference in rank, the second the complete first-person account on the record\"."
  }
 ],
 "overall": "The final draft's facts, dates, quotations, attributions and Chinese–English parity all match the evidence, and the interpretive passages are labelled as interpretation. The only problem is in the analysis section's counterfactual paragraph, which assigns both consequences to both imagined arrangements; it needs a small wording fix (fix) before publication."
}